= Solution
{parent=i}

For a <martingale> and a <stopping time> $T\leq N$, expand the stopped value using its <martingale differences>:
$$
M_T-M_0=\sum_{k=1}^N\mathbf1_{\{T\geq k\}}(M_k-M_{k-1}).
$$
The event $\{T\geq k\}=\{T>k-1\}$ belongs to the <filtration> $\mathcal F_{k-1}$. Each summand is integrable and its <conditional expectation> given $\mathcal F_{k-1}$ is zero. Taking <expectations> of this finite sum therefore proves **$\mathbb E M_T=\mathbb E M_0$ for every bounded stopping time**. This proves the forward direction of the <bounded-stopping-time characterization of a martingale> without assuming an <optional stopping theorem>.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For $A\in\mathcal F_n$, define the bounded <stopping time> $T=n+1$ on $A$ and $T=n$ on $A^c$. Before time $n$ its stopping events are empty, at time $n$ the event $\{T\leq n\}=A^c$ belongs to $\mathcal F_n$, and subsequently it has stopped everywhere. Subtract the assumed mean identity for this $T$ from that for the constant <stopping time> $n$ to obtain
$$
\mathbb E\bigl[\mathbf1_A(M_{n+1}-M_n)\bigr]=0\qquad(A\in\mathcal F_n).
$$
Since $M_n$ is integrable and $\mathcal F_n$-<measurable>, this is precisely the defining property of <conditional expectation> giving **$\mathbb E[M_{n+1}\mid\mathcal F_n]=M_n$**. Thus the <adapted process> is a <martingale>, completing the <bounded-stopping-time characterization of a martingale>.

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The required condition is <uniform integrability>, written entirely in terms of the given <probability laws>:
$$
\boxed{\lim_{R\to\infty}\sup_n\int_{\{|x|>R\}}|x|\,\mu_n(dx)=0.}
$$
Together with the given <almost sure convergence>, this implies <L1 convergence> to an integrable $M_\infty$. Passing to the limit in $\mathbb E[M_m\mid\mathcal F_n]=M_n$, using the contraction property of <conditional expectation> in <L1 space>, gives $M_n=\mathbb E[M_\infty\mid\mathcal F_n]$.

For every bounded <stopping time> $S$, the same identity holds with $\mathcal F_S$: split an event of its <stopping-time sigma-algebra> according to $\{S=k\}$ and use the deterministic-time identity. In particular $M_{T\wedge n}=\mathbb E[M_\infty\mid\mathcal F_{T\wedge n}]$. These <conditional expectations> are <uniformly integrable>: for an integrable $Y$ and $Z=\mathbb E[Y\mid\mathcal G]$, the event $A=\{|Z|>K\}$ satisfies $\mathbb P(A)\leq\mathbb E|Y|/K$, and
$$
\mathbb E[|Z|;A]\leq\mathbb E[|Y|;|Y|>R]+R\mathbb E|Y|/K.
$$
First choose $R$, then $K$, uniformly in $\mathcal G$. Define $M_T=M_\infty$ on $\{T=\infty\}$. Now $M_{T\wedge n}\to M_T$ <almost surely> and in <L1 space>, so the bounded-time mean identities imply **$\mathbb E M_T=\mathbb E M_0$ even when $T$ can be infinite**.

= c
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

With the natural <filtration> $\mathcal F_n=\sigma(Z_1,\ldots,Z_n)$, the sums form a <martingale>. Put $v_n=\sum_{k\leq n}a_k^2$. <Independence> and the <characteristic function> of a <standard normal random variable> give
$$
\mathbb E e^{iuM_n}=e^{-u^2v_n/2}.
$$
If $M_n\to M_\infty$ <almost surely> with a finite limit, <dominated convergence> gives convergence of these <characteristic functions> to $\mathbb E e^{iuM_\infty}$. If $v_n\to\infty$, that limit would be $1$ at zero and $0$ at every nonzero $u$. A <characteristic function> is continuous at zero: apply <dominated convergence> to $e^{iuM_\infty}$ as $u\to0$. This contradiction proves
$$
\boxed{\sum_{k=1}^\infty a_k^2<\infty.}
$$
This is also sufficient by the <L2-bounded martingale convergence theorem>, since $\mathbb EM_n^2=v_n$. Thus the <almost sure convergence criterion for an independent Gaussian series> is an equivalence.

= d
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

The exact condition is again **$\sum_ka_k^2<\infty$**, with the terminal almost-sure limit used to define $M_T$ when $T=\infty$. In that case $\sup_n\mathbb EM_n^2<\infty$, and
$$
\mathbb E[|M_n|;|M_n|>R]\leq\frac{\mathbb EM_n^2}{R}
$$
proves <uniform integrability>. The preceding unbounded <optional stopping> argument yields $\boxed{\mathbb E M_T=0}$.

For necessity, suppose $v_n\to\infty$. For every fixed $R>0$, the <normal distribution> of $M_n$ gives $\mathbb P(M_n\geq R)\to1/2$. Hence $\mathbb P(\sup_nM_n\geq R)\geq1/2$, and intersecting over positive integer $R$ shows $\mathbb P(\sup_nM_n=\infty)\geq1/2$. Unboundedness above is unchanged by altering finitely many of the <independent> $Z_k$, because that changes all sufficiently late sums by one finite constant. It is therefore a <tail event>; the <Kolmogorov zero-one law> makes its probability one. Consequently $T<\infty$ <almost surely> and $M_T\geq1$. Its <expectation> is at least one, possibly infinite, and cannot equal zero. This proves the necessity assertion in <threshold stopping of an independent Gaussian series>.

